/-!
# Source development for recognizing simple groups of type J₄

The current exports construct the faithful action on Q/Z(Q), a cubic Sylow
complement with its exact quotient map, and H = Q N_H(X), starting from the
intrinsic type-J₄ hypotheses. They also promote each local Sylow two-subgroup
to an ambient Sylow subgroup, proving source (4.5), and provide the input
conversion from a self-centralizing two-core used by the quasithin consumer.

The orthogonal binary action excludes the simple M₂₂ cover, including its
potentially reducible actions. The actual centralizer C_{H/⟨z⟩}(X) is
identified with the native triple cover through the original quotient maps.
Its action on Q/Z(Q) is the native six-dimensional GF(4) module, and
C_Q(X) = Z(Q). These source (4.1) conclusions are unconditional from IsTypeJ4.
The native vector-orbit calculation now preserves the original square and
polar forms and identifies the full source H/Q action with the native cubic
extension. Its
two noncentral involution representatives lie in different H-conjugacy classes
and cover all such classes. Their quotient-vector stabilizers are the actual
element-centralizer images through H → H/Q and have orders 3840 and 1920.
Their native identifications retain the cubic covering kernel and the exact
quotient maps; the original projection is injective on these stabilizers. The
returning cores now have reciprocal membership and elementary intersection
of order 128. Their literal crossing image is normal elementary of order 32
in the original quotient stabilizer. This proves the intersection-rank clause
of (4.3) without assuming the stabilizer types. The first stabilizer is now
identified with a genuine full-design pair stabilizer: its intrinsic two-core
is elementary of order 32 and its actual quotient is S₅. A checked action on
five involutions identifies the second stabilizer's elementary order-16 core
and S₅ quotient, completing (4.2). The order-32 crossing image excludes this
second class from returning fusion, proving (4.3)(1), and is exactly the
two-core of the literal stabilizer. The original core intersections now
construct the elementary three-space of order eight in (4.3)(2), exactly
generated by three distinct conjugate markers. The proof identifies the
unique singular six-space and its scalar intersection using checked native
quadratic identities, then transports them through the original center
quotient. The original cubic commutator now generates that same three-space
with z. Its full normalizer fuses all seven nonzero points, and their original
cores contain and normalize it. Their join K is exactly the source conjugate
join and is relatively normal. The actual conjugation images of N_G(Z) and K
are all Aut(Z); the literal kernel R=K∩C_G(Z) therefore gives K/R≅L₃(2),
and every normalizer element is an actual product from K and S=C_G(Z).
Each nonzero point core meets S in a normal two-subgroup of order 2¹¹.
Two such intersections generate an order-2¹⁵ subgroup inside O₂(R),
[K,S] lies in O₂(R), and O₂(K)=O₂(R).
All these local data are constructed from IsTypeJ4, with the original cubic
quotient and Sylow retained. The special-core, S/R and tensor clauses,
later point/line groups, comparison and triangulability remain unproved;
this module does not yet export the type-J₄ recognition theorem.

Source: Aschbacher–Segev, *The uniqueness of groups of type J₄*, Invent.
Math. 105 (1991), 589–607, DOI 10.1007/BF01232280, especially §4.
-/
